.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "why", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. Why Now
  :markdown
    Two things changed. **Solid Cache** is the default cache store for new
    Rails 8 applications: it keeps entries in a database table, so there is
    no Redis or Memcached to provision, and because disk is cheap the cache
    can be enormous and long-lived — entries stay warm for days instead of
    being evicted by lunchtime. **Hotwire** moved rendering back to the
    server. A Turbo app answers every navigation, frame load, and stream
    with server-rendered HTML, which means the same fragments are generated
    over and over for every visitor. That is exactly the workload a fragment
    cache exists for.

    The trade has not changed: a cache turns a rendering problem into an
    invalidation problem. Everything below is about keeping the second
    problem small.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "fragments", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. Fragment and Russian-Doll Caching
  :markdown
    The `cache` helper wraps a block of view logic. The first request
    renders it and writes the HTML to the store; every later request reads
    it back. The key combines the record's `cache_key_with_version` with a
    **digest of the template tree**, so editing the template (or any partial
    it renders) busts the entry without you doing anything:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      -# app/views/products/index.html.haml
      - @products.each do |product|
        - cache product do
          = render product

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    **Russian-doll** caching is nesting: an outer cache for the list, inner
    caches for each item. When one item changes, only its own fragment and
    the outer fragment re-render — the other items are read straight out of
    the cache and stitched in. For that to work the parent's version has to
    change when a child does, which is what `touch: true` is for:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/models/review.rb
      class Review < ApplicationRecord
        belongs_to :product, touch: true   # a new or edited review bumps the product's updated_at
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      -# app/views/products/show.html.haml
      - cache @product do
        %h1= @product.name

        - @product.reviews.each do |review|
          - cache review do
            = render review

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    `cache_if` and `cache_unless` take a condition first, for fragments
    that should only be cached for some visitors — the classic case is a
    fragment that renders admin controls:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      - cache_unless current_user&.admin?, product do
        = render product

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "collections", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Collection Caching
  :markdown
    Wrapping each item of a list in its own `cache` block works, but it
    reads the cache once per item. Rendering the collection with
    `cached: true` fetches every cached entry in **one multi-read**, renders
    only the misses, and writes those back for next time:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      = render partial: "products/product", collection: @products, cached: true

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Two caveats. The whole collection must render through **one partial**
    (Rails needs a single template digest for the batch), so a list that
    switches partials per item cannot use it. And the cache key is the item
    alone by default: if the rendering depends on anything else — the
    locale, the viewer's role — fold it into the key with a lambda, or pass
    the options hash for an expiry:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      = render partial: "products/product", collection: @products,
        cached: ->(product) { [I18n.locale, product] }

      = render partial: "products/product", collection: @products,
        cached: { expires_in: 1.hour, key: ->(product) { [I18n.locale, product] } }

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "keys", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Cache Keys and Versioning
  :markdown
    Modern Rails uses **recyclable cache keys**. A record's `cache_key` is
    stable (`products/42`), and its *version* — derived from `updated_at` —
    is stored inside the entry. When the product is touched, the version no
    longer matches and the entry is overwritten in place rather than
    orphaned under a new key. That is why `touch: true` is the whole
    invalidation strategy for most apps: bump `updated_at` and every
    fragment that depends on the record is stale.

    Anything else the fragment depends on has to be in the key
    explicitly. Pass an array — records, strings, whatever changes the
    output:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      -# The fragment shows a price in the visitor's currency and differs for admins.
      - cache [product, current_currency, current_user&.admin?] do
        = render product

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    The **template tree digest** covers the other half: Rails scans each
    template for `render` calls and hashes the whole tree, so editing a
    nested partial invalidates every fragment that includes it. It can only
    see literal `render` calls, though. When a render hides inside a helper
    method, or the partial name is built at runtime, declare the dependency
    with a comment so the digest stays honest:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      -# Template Dependency: todolists/todolist
      = render_sortable_todolists @project.todolists

      -# Template Dependency: events/*
      = render_categorizable_events @person.events

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "http", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 5. HTTP and Conditional Caching
  :markdown
    Fragment caching saves rendering; **conditional GET** saves the whole
    response. `fresh_when` sets `ETag` and `Last-Modified` headers from a
    record, and when the browser sends them back on the next request and
    nothing has changed, Rails answers **304 Not Modified** with an empty
    body — no view is rendered at all:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/controllers/products_controller.rb
      def show
        @product = Product.find(params[:id])
        fresh_when @product   # ETag + Last-Modified from cache_key_with_version and updated_at
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    `stale?` is the same check as a conditional, for actions that need to
    do more than render when the record has changed. And `expires_in` sets
    `Cache-Control` so a browser or CDN can serve the response without
    asking at all — reserve it for genuinely public, slow-changing pages:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      def show
        @product = Product.find(params[:id])

        if stale?(@product)
          respond_to do |format|
            format.html
            format.json { render json: @product }
          end
        end
      end

      def pricing
        expires_in 1.hour, public: true
      end

= doc_note(modifier: :note, title: "ETags and Layouts", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    Rails generates *weak* ETags by default and mixes in the template
    digests, so an ETag changes when the layout does. Anything else that
    varies the page — the current user, a flash message — must be folded in
    with `fresh_when @product, etag: [current_user, flash]`, or the browser
    will happily show one user another's 304.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "components", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 6. Caching ViewComponents
  :markdown
    This stack renders through ViewComponent, and components have a
    caching blind spot: the template tree digest is built from *templates*,
    and a component is a Ruby class. Wrap a component render in a `cache`
    block and editing the component's template, class, or sidecar files
    **does not** invalidate the fragment — stale markup ships until the
    cache is cleared by hand.

    ViewComponent 4.14 fixes this with `ViewComponent::ExperimentallyCacheable`.
    Include it in a component and the component joins Rails' digest tree:
    the surrounding fragment is invalidated when the component's template,
    Ruby class, sidecar files, superclasses, child components, or rendered
    partials change.

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/components/product_card_component.rb
      class ProductCardComponent < ApplicationComponent
        include ViewComponent::ExperimentallyCacheable

        def initialize(product:)
          @product = product
        end
      end

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "haml") do
    :plain
      - cache product do
        = render ProductCardComponent.new(product: product)

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    A component can also cache **itself**, so every call site is cached
    without a `cache` block. `cache_on` names the methods whose values form
    the key; the digest, format, variant, and locale are added for you, and
    `if:` / `unless:` skip the cache for some renders:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      class ProductCardComponent < ApplicationComponent
        include ViewComponent::ExperimentallyCacheable

        cache_on :product, unless: -> { product.draft? }

        def initialize(product:)
          @product = product
        end

        private

        attr_reader :product
      end

= doc_note(modifier: :warning, title: "Self-Caching Components Can't Take Content", css: "mt-6") do
  :markdown
    A block, `with_content`, or a slot filled by the caller is not part of
    the cache key, so passing one to a `cache_on` component raises
    `ContentPassedToCachedComponentError` — in development and test too,
    not just when caching is on. Move the content into the component and
    derive it from `cache_on` values, and declare `cache_on` on the
    components that should self-cache, never on `ApplicationComponent`
    (it is inherited). Note the API is marked **experimental** and may
    change in a minor release.

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Two more things the digest cannot see: a component rendered from a
    runtime value (`render @component`) and a module included into a
    component (modules have no template to hash). Declare both with the
    same `Template Dependency:` comment, naming components by class. And
    when a cache is written somewhere the component isn't rendered — a
    background job precomputing a summary — `ProductCardComponent.cache_digest`
    ties the entry to the component's source.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "turbo", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 7. Turbo and Hotwire
  :markdown
    Fragment caches and Turbo are good neighbours because Turbo only ever
    asks for HTML. A **Turbo Frame** that lazy-loads (`src:` with
    `loading: "lazy"`) is a separate GET, so the frame's action gets
    `fresh_when` and its template gets `cache` blocks like any other page —
    and because a lazy frame is often the slow, reusable part of a page (a
    sidebar, a recommendations panel), it is usually the *best* place to
    cache. **Turbo Streams** broadcast partials, and a `cache` block inside
    a broadcast partial behaves exactly as it does in a request. **Page
    refreshes with morphing** re-render the whole page on every change,
    which is precisely when Russian-doll caching pays off: only the changed
    fragment costs anything.

    The one rule: keep **per-visitor** markup out of cached fragments. A
    CSRF token, a "signed in as" badge, or a Stimulus target that depends
    on the current user inside a `cache` block will be served to the next
    visitor. Render those outside the block, or key the block on the user.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "stores", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 8. Stores and Configuration
  :markdown
    Everything above writes through `Rails.cache`, and the store behind it
    is a one-line choice per environment.

- # The store table: the store, what it is good for, and the catch.
.mt-4.overflow-x-auto.rounded-box.border.border-base-300
