.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    A new Rails 8 app already has Solid Cache wired up; an existing app
    adds the gem and runs its installer, which writes the migration,
    `config/cache.yml`, and the `cache` database entry. Then point
    production at it:

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4") do |code|
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bundle add solid_cache
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bin/rails solid_cache:install

.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # config/environments/production.rb
      config.cache_store = :solid_cache_store

.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(css: "my-4", language: "yaml") do
    :plain
      # config/cache.yml
      default: &default
        store_options:
          max_age: <%= 60.days.to_i %>      # retention ceiling
          max_size: <%= 256.megabytes %>    # FIFO eviction past this
          namespace: <%= Rails.env %>

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    In **development** caching is off by default so you see every change
    immediately. Rails toggles it with `bin/rails dev:cache`, which creates
    or removes `tmp/caching-dev.txt`; LocoMotion's demo wraps the same file
    in `just demo-cache` / `just demo-nocache`. Turn it on when you are
    working on cache behaviour, off when you are working on views.

.max-w-prose
  = daisy_code(css: "my-4") do |code|
    - code.with_line(prefix: "$") do
      bin/rails dev:cache
    - code.with_line(prefix: ">") do
      Development mode is now being cached.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "when-not", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 9. When Not to Cache
  :markdown
    Every cache block is a place where the page can be wrong. It earns that
    risk only when the fragment is **slow to render**, **shown often**, and
    **the same for many visitors**. Skip it when:

    - The fragment is cheap. A few dozen tags with no queries render faster
      than the cache read that would replace them.
    - The fragment is personalised. If the key has to include the current
      user, the hit rate is one — you are storing a copy of every page for
      every visitor and reusing none of them.
    - The data changes more often than it is viewed. A live dashboard
      invalidates faster than it can be read back.
    - You have not measured. Profile the page first — the slow part is
      usually a query, and a query fix helps every visitor without adding
      an invalidation problem to maintain.

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "success", class: "scroll-mt-24" } Success!
  :markdown
    You know the modern caching toolkit and, more importantly, where each
    piece belongs. Before you move on:

